          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An eight-year-old boy presents with fever, jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, and ma</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An eight-year-old boy presents with fever, jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, and markedly elevated transaminases after exposure to a contaminated communal water supply. Which single test would confirm acute hepatitis A infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>IgG anti-HAV</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>IgG anti-HAV indicates past infection or vaccination-induced immunity and remains positive lifelong. It cannot confirm a current acute infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IgM anti-HAV</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. IgM anti-HAV appears early in acute hepatitis A infection and confirms current active disease, distinguishing it from prior exposure indicated by IgG.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>HBsAg</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HBsAg is the surface antigen of hepatitis B virus. It identifies hepatitis B infection, not hepatitis A, which is a different virus transmitted by the faeco-oral route.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ALT level</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ALT is a non-specific marker of hepatocellular injury elevated in many conditions — viral, toxic, metabolic. It cannot identify the causative virus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the distinction: IgM antibodies indicate acute (current) infection, while IgG indicates past exposure or immunity. For hepatitis A specifically, IgM anti-HAV is the confirmatory test."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. IgM anti-HAV confirms acute hepatitis A. The faeco-oral exposure through contaminated water, self-limiting course, and absence of chronic sequelae are characteristic of hepatitis A."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
