          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In a child with chronic liver disease, which of the following clinical signs res</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In a child with chronic liver disease, which of the following clinical signs results from hepatic insufficiency rather than portal hypertension?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Splenomegaly</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Splenomegaly in CLD results from portal venous congestion causing splenic engorgement — it is a direct consequence of raised portal pressure, not of hepatic synthetic failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spider angiomata on the chest</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Spider angiomata develop because the failing liver cannot adequately metabolise oestrogen, leading to arteriolar dilatation — a hallmark of hepatic insufficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Caput medusae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Caput medusae represents dilated periumbilical veins that form as portosystemic collaterals when portal venous pressure is raised — a classic sign of portal hypertension.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oesophageal varices</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oesophageal varices are portosystemic collateral vessels that develop when raised portal pressure diverts blood through alternative venous channels. They are a direct consequence of portal hypertension.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "CLD produces two overlapping processes: hepatic insufficiency (jaundice, spider angiomata, palmar erythema, coagulopathy, hypoalbuminaemia) and portal hypertension (splenomegaly, varices, ascites, caput medusae). Spider angiomata belong to the insufficiency group."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Spider angiomata, palmar erythema, and gynaecomastia all result from impaired hepatic oestrogen metabolism — they are markers of hepatic insufficiency, distinct from the signs of portal hypertension."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
