          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A ten-year-old child with portal hypertension presents with haematemesis from oe</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A ten-year-old child with portal hypertension presents with haematemesis from oesophageal varices. After securing IV access, what haemoglobin target should guide blood transfusion?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>5–6 g/dL</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This level is dangerously low and risks tissue hypoxia. While restrictive transfusion is the goal, the target must still maintain adequate oxygen delivery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>7–8 g/dL</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A restrictive target of 7–8 g/dL maintains adequate oxygen delivery while avoiding the rise in portal pressure that over-transfusion causes, which can trigger re-bleeding from varices.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>10–12 g/dL (near-normal)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Transfusing to near-normal haemoglobin levels raises portal venous pressure and significantly increases the risk of re-bleeding. This is why restrictive transfusion is standard in variceal bleeding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Transfusion is contraindicated in variceal bleeding</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Transfusion is not contraindicated — it is often necessary for haemodynamic stability. However, it must be given to a restrictive target of 7–8 g/dL to avoid raising portal pressure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Over-transfusion in variceal bleeding raises portal pressure and can trigger re-bleeding. The evidence supports a restrictive haemoglobin target of 7–8 g/dL — enough for oxygenation, without the dangerous pressure spike."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Restrictive transfusion to 7–8 g/dL is evidence-based practice in variceal bleeding — it balances oxygen delivery against the risk of pressure-driven re-bleeding."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
