          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>While examining a child with suspected chronic liver disease, you percuss from t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>While examining a child with suspected chronic liver disease, you percuss from the umbilicus toward the flank and find a tympanic-to-dull transition. After rolling the child onto their side, the dull area shifts to the newly dependent side. What does this finding confirm?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatomegaly extending across the midline</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Liver dullness is fixed in position and does not shift when the patient is repositioned. A dull boundary that moves with posture indicates freely mobile material, not a solid organ edge.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Free peritoneal fluid (ascites)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Shifting dullness confirms that fluid in the peritoneal cavity moves freely with gravity. When the child rolls, the fluid settles to the dependent side, shifting the tympanic-dull boundary accordingly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Massive splenomegaly crossing the midline</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Splenic dullness is fixed in the left upper quadrant and does not shift with repositioning. A boundary that moves with posture indicates free fluid, not an enlarged solid organ.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A distended urinary bladder</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bladder distension produces a suprapubic dull area that is midline, does not shift with lateral repositioning, and resolves after catheterisation — quite distinct from the shifting dullness of ascites.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Shifting dullness tests for free peritoneal fluid (ascites). Unlike organ-related dullness, which stays fixed, free fluid moves with gravity, causing the tympanic-dull boundary to shift when the patient changes position."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Shifting dullness is the classic bedside test for free peritoneal fluid. The principle is straightforward — fluid follows gravity, so the dull area moves to whichever side becomes dependent."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
